{"t": 85.939, "n": 45, "ev": "result", "ok": true, "text": "I replaced the incident values in `regeneration-failures.spec.md` with generic reports. I made no other change. I ran no structure check or judge, so the gate decides whether the finding is closed.\n\n- **RGF-B3, third scenario (line 30):** it no longer names `V4 audit` or `outcome-unverified-blocker`. It now reads: \"GIVEN an `upgrade-check` report with one failed check that gives evidence for the failure WHEN the regeneration fails THEN one finding holds that check and that evidence\".\n- **RGF-AC3 (line 75):** the two fixture lists are now generic reports.\n  - `author verify`: one whose failed rung lists one finding, and one whose failed rung lists two.\n  - `upgrade-check`: one with one failed check and its evidence, and one with two failed checks.\n  - The rest of the line is unchanged, from \"each give one finding for each failure listed\" to the end.\n\nA search of the spec finds none of the incident values (`V4 audit`, `outcome-unverified`, `60 characters`, \"no pass case\", \"no fail case\", \"no case verifies\", \"summary over\"). No id, behaviour or criterion changed.\n\nI left the hand-off alone. It doesn't quote the two spec lines. Line 57 points to the engine files that show the report shapes. Line 70 is an open question that names the engine's real report codes, `blueprint-difference` and `outcome-unverified-blocker`, and the task's own values belong there.\n\nI kept `compare` and `blueprint-difference` in the first RGF-B3 scenario (line 28), since you scoped the fix to line 30 and RGF-AC3. If the judge counts those as real-run values too, they are the next thing to make generic.\n\nI also opened all six rule files with the read tool. I read no code, capability list or other spec.\n\nARTIFACT handoff: docs/tasks/i203a.handoff.md", "subtype": "success", "is_error": false, "session_id": "f1f77b55-a08e-44c5-bf2a-ee0715604be9"}
